.TH TELNETD 1
.SH NAME
telnetd \- accept remote shells on pseudo-terminals
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B telnetd
.RI [ -p " PORT]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
Listen on TCP port 23, or the specified port. Run telnetd & to retain the
local shell. Each connection gets a new controlling PTY and /bin/sh session,
with terminal echo, signals, window dimensions and shell job control.
Exit the remote shell to disconnect; shutdown stops the whole guest.
.PP
This service grants an unauthenticated root shell. Traffic is plaintext.
Use only on a trusted host-only network. It does not start automatically.
.PP
Four PTY pairs and four TCP connections are shared with other applications.
Each connected shell also needs a bridge process, so the kernel process
table can limit concurrent sessions. Disconnecting hangs up the remote
terminal. Files use the ordinary persistent filesystem.
.SH SEE ALSO
telnet(1), ptysh(1), sh(1), stty(1)
